WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.213
This is one of the most important lessons. Recursion, and the call stack that makes it work.

00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:08.676
Imagine Russian nesting dolls.

00:00:09.126 --> 00:00:14.316
To find the smallest doll, you open one, and inside is a smaller version of the same problem.

00:00:14.766 --> 00:00:19.026
You keep opening until you hit the tiny solid doll. That is the base case.

00:00:19.476 --> 00:00:23.981
Then you close the dolls back up in reverse order. That is the function returning.

00:00:25.331 --> 00:00:31.438
Every function call gets a frame on the call stack, holding its local variables and where to return.

00:00:31.888 --> 00:00:34.291
Let us compute factorial of three.

00:00:34.741 --> 00:00:39.873
Factorial of three calls factorial of two. A new frame is pushed on top.

00:00:40.323 --> 00:00:44.108
Factorial of two calls factorial of one. Another frame.

00:00:44.558 --> 00:00:48.401
Factorial of one is the base case. It returns one.

00:00:48.851 --> 00:00:53.495
Now frames pop off: two times one is two, then three times two is six.

00:00:54.845 --> 00:00:57.782
On a tree, recursion follows the branches.

00:00:58.232 --> 00:01:02.899
To validate a binary search tree, each call receives an allowed range.

00:01:03.349 --> 00:01:09.026
Going left, the upper limit shrinks to the parent value. Going right, the lower limit rises.

00:01:09.476 --> 00:01:13.459
Watch the stack grow as we go down, and shrink as calls return.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:19.458
At node three, the range is five to six, and three is outside it. So the answer is false.

00:01:20.808 --> 00:01:27.658
Every recursive function needs two parts: a base case that stops, and a step that makes the problem smaller.

00:01:28.108 --> 00:01:34.018
The call stack has limited size. A recursion depth of a million can crash with a stack overflow.

00:01:34.468 --> 00:01:39.773
In kernel code the stack is only a few kilobytes, so recursion is usually avoided there.

00:01:40.223 --> 00:01:44.240
You can always convert recursion into a loop with your own explicit stack.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.039
Backtracking is recursion that undoes its last choice.

00:01:49.489 --> 00:01:53.865
Choose an option, explore deeper, then un-choose it before trying the next one.

00:01:54.315 --> 00:01:58.681
Subsets, permutations, and N queens all follow this template.

00:02:00.031 --> 00:02:06.718
In this course every recursive problem has an execution visualizer. Press step and watch frames push and pop.
